### Place 1 – Kodaikanal

First, let's start with Kodaikanal. How many of you have visited Kodaikanal, except those from Tamil Nadu?

_(Pause.)_

That's nice\! You already know how beautiful this place is\!

Kodaikanal is located in the Palani Hills. It is famous for its cool climate, misty mountains, green trees, and peaceful atmosphere.
For people like us, who live in hot cities like Chennai, Kodaikanal is the perfect place to escape the heat and enjoy the cool weather.
Some popular attractions are Kodaikanal Lake, Coaker's Walk, Pillar Rocks, and Guna Caves, which became famous through the movie _Manjummel Boys_.

One of my favourite experiences was cycling around Kodaikanal Lake. We rented bicycles and enjoyed riding around the lake. The cool breeze, fresh eucalyptus smell, and beautiful surroundings made the experience unforgettable.

Kodaikanal is also famous for homemade chocolates and the Kurinji flower, which blooms approximately once every 12 years.

For me, Kodaikanal is the perfect place to relax and refresh our minds.

### Place 2 – Madurai

Now, let's move on to Madurai. Has anyone here visited Madurai?

_(Pause.)_

Great\! Madurai is one of the oldest cities in Tamil Nadu and is popularly known as the Temple City.

Its most famous attraction is the Meenakshi Amman Temple, known for its 14 colourful towers and beautiful sculptures.

Madurai is also famous for its grand Chithirai Festival, celebrated with great excitement.Every year during
the Chithirai Festival, the whole city celebrates the divine wedding of Meenakshi and
Sundareshwarar, and lakhs of people join in.

But for me, Madurai is not just about temples and history; it's also about food\!

I love its famous Jigarthanda, Karupatti coffee, and cottenseed milk known as Paruthi Paal. I also enjoy its simple lifestyle and peaceful atmosphere, which makes me feel relaxed away from busy city life.
That's what makes Madurai special to me.
From those temples came Bharatanatyam. Look at the dancer on the screen. Every hand
gesture, every eye movement and every pose follows precise geometry. It's storytelling through
the body, telling great epics without a single spoken word.

Then comes Pongal, Tamil Nadu's harvest thanksgiving. Families boil fresh rice and milk in clay
pots until they overflow, and everyone shouts "Pongalo Pongal!" The overflow is a symbol of
prosperity and gratitude to the Sun.
